I am currently working on register allocation for both my register based
bytecode virtual machine and my x86 just in time compiler, both for my
programming language purple-garden .

Register allocation is assigning the unlimited amount of virtual registers to
the limited set of storage resources hardware has, specifically the gprs
(general purpose registers), the fprs (floating point registers) and the stack
(see CPU Registers x86 for an
examplary overview). The first step in this odeal is livelyness analsis.

1 fn f ( a : Int n : Int acc : Int ) Int { 
 2 match { 
 3 n < 1 { a + acc } 
 4 { f ( a n - 1 acc * n ) } 
 5 } 
 6 } This is equiv to the following c code, which i &ldquo;disassembled&rdquo;
from the very well written Linear scan register allocation on
SSA article by Max
Bernstein 1 , in which i didnt really understand the
IR display, before translating it to c and then to my own
language (purple garden does not have loops 🤓 so this is only
equiv when called with f(a n 1) ):

1 long f ( long a , long n ) { 
 2 long acc = 1 ; 
 3 while ( n >= 1 ) { 
 4 acc = acc * n ; 
 5 n = n - 1 ; 
 6 } 
 7 return a + acc ; 
 8 } Feeding f(a,n,acc: Int): Int into purple gardens pipeline
produces the following, optimised immediate representation
(using -I ), this means some values were dead and thus removed 2 :

1 fn f 1 ( %v0 :Int , %v1 :Int , %v2 :Int ) - > Int { 
 2 b 0 ( %v0 , %v1 , %v2 ) : 
 3 %v4 :Bool = ILt %v1 , 1 
 4 br %v4 , b 1 ( %v0 , %v1 , %v2 ), b 2 ( %v0 , %v1 , %v2 ) 
 5 b 1 ( %v5 , %v6 , %v7 ) : 
 6 %v8 :Int = IAdd %v5 , %v7 
 7 ret %v8 
 8 b 2 ( %v9 , %v10 , %v11 ) : 
 9 %v13 :Int = ISub %v10 , 1 
 10 %v14 :Int = IMul %v11 , %v10 
 11 tail f 1 ( %v9 , %v13 , %v14 ) 
 12 } PUrple garden IR structure and notation Purple gardens immediate representation is static single assigment
